  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1117 PM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nLows tonight are forecast to be similar to the last few nights with \ntemperatures around freezing for the plains and in the upper teens \nto 20s for the high country. The weak trough of interest for \nFriday's weather is over the western US at this hour and moving \ntowards Colorado, but it is forecast to split into two. Each \nsuccessive suite of model runs has trended the trough that is moving \ncloser to Colorado weaker and to the north of our area. \nConsequently, the 18z and 00z short-term models have trended even \ndrier for our area. For our high country, the HRRR shows almost no \nprecipitation with the RRFS showing very light snow showers and \nisolated coverage at best. Thus, light snow chances remain low (10-\n20%) for locations above 8,500 feet on Friday afternoon and evening. \nAny snow accumulation is forecast to be 1 inch or less with the vast \nmajority (> 80%) of the high country not seeing any accumulation. \nAny precipitation will quickly end on Friday evening. Highs for the \nplains on Friday afternoon are forecast to be near seasonal averages-\nin the mid to upper 50s. Some light northeast winds and increased \ncloud cover could lower temperatures a few degrees from the current \nforecast.\n\nFor Saturday through at least the end of the forecast period, \nridging aloft builds in taking firm control of our weather. Above \naverage temperatures (but below records), mostly sunny skies, and \nprecipitation chances near zero continue to be expected for the week \nahead. The mid-level ridge looks strongest for our area on Sunday \nand Monday with temperatures pushing 7 to 8 C at 700 mb. As noted \nover the last few days, ensemble guidance shows high temperatures in \nthe mid to upper 60s for the urban corridor and highs approaching 70 \nF on the far eastern plains for those days. The other days this week \ndo still look to remain above average with highs generally in the \nlow to mid 60s on Saturday as well as Tuesday through at least \nThursday for the plains. The ridge could break down by the end of \nnext week into next weekend, but there is poor agreement on when \nexactly this will take place at this time.",
